According to a new report from Intel Market Research, the global white goods market was valued at USD 204.08 billion in 2024 and is projected to reach USD 259.41 billion by 2032, growing at a CAGR of 3.6% during the forecast period (2025–2032). This steady growth trajectory reflects rising demand for household appliances across both developed and emerging economies, fueled by urbanization, technological advancements, and increasing consumer purchasing power.
What are White Goods?
White goods refer to large household appliances traditionally finished in white enamel, though modern variants now include stainless steel and other finishes. These essential domestic appliances - including refrigerators, washing machines, air conditioners, ovens, and dishwashers - play critical roles in daily household operations and quality of life improvements. The sector has evolved significantly with smart technology integration, energy efficiency improvements, and changing consumer lifestyles driving product innovation.
